  "slug": "edna-diversity-monkfish-759e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test how seawater storage and specimen-based sequencing affect molecular biodiversity estimates from eDNA and museum material.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread in this set is molecular inference of biodiversity from environmental samples and specimens. A freezing experiment on seawater samples reports that one month of storage lowered eDNA-inferred marine invertebrate richness and altered community composition, with Pseudocalanus copepods among the notably affected taxa, pointing to preservation as a source of bias in metabarcoding estimates. A Cook Inlet time series applies eDNA metabarcoding and qPCR to track endangered beluga whale occurrence alongside salmonid and eulachon prey dynamics. Nanopore barcoding of 3,657 European Ulva samples identifies 46 species from 30 countries, and nanopore mini-barcoding recovers COI sequences from century-old spider museum specimens. What is new is direct attention to sampling protocol effects and to multi-taxon eDNA time series, alongside low-input genotyping of zebra mussel veligers via multiple displacement amplification.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
